WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.331
Scenario: An organization has two alerts: (A) malware is

2
00:00:03.331 --> 00:00:06.662
actively encrypting files on a shared operational server; (B)

3
00:00:06.662 --> 00:00:09.994
a single employee received a suspicious email but did

4
00:00:09.994 --> 00:00:13.325
not open it. Both require triage. Safest response: Option

5
00:00:13.325 --> 00:00:16.656
D: Prioritize the active encryption event for immediate coordinated

6
00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:19.987
containment while ensuring the email is reported and triaged.

7
00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:23.318
Active disruption and potential spread represent immediate operational risk;

8
00:00:23.318 --> 00:00:26.649
the lower-impact email still needs handling. Option A is

9
00:00:26.649 --> 00:00:30.351
unsafe because Delaying containment during active encryption may increase damage.

10
00:00:30.351 --> 00:00:33.682
Option B is unsafe because General prevalence does not

11
00:00:33.682 --> 00:00:37.013
outweigh evidence of active encryption and immediate operational impact.

12
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:40.344
Option C is unsafe because Incidents differ in impact,

13
00:00:40.344 --> 00:00:44.045
scope, active harm and urgency; equal priority is not risk-based.

14
00:00:44.045 --> 00:00:47.377
In practice: Activate the incident-response plan, coordinate containment of

15
00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:50.708
the actively affected system, assign triage to the email

16
00:00:50.708 --> 00:00:54.039
and document priority rationale. Key takeaway: Prioritization considers current

17
00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:57.000
impact, scope, exposure, time sensitivity and containment opportunity.
